/-!
# Source applicability and the central four-group in 13.6.6

The marked configuration supplies the actual Sylow subgroup of C_G(v).
A component meets this Sylow subgroup noncentrally: otherwise transfer and
perfectness make that intersection trivial, and the component is forced
into the trivial odd core. This discharges the normalization test for a
and z, including centrality only modulo the line generated by v.

Characteristic two of C_G(z) follows from even characteristic. Conjugacy
and relative centralizer inheritance give the corresponding local assertion
for a. The full earlier conjugacy alternative A.3.8(3), lifted through the
two-core quotient by adequate 1.2.1(3), makes O²(C_L(v)) normalize K.
The marked product constructs z in the central supplement of that residual.
Together these prove all three commutator identities and construct the
actual four-group in the faithful component action quotient.

`Setup` consists of preceding source data. The universal A.3.8 assertion
remains explicit. This is the applicability part of the initial sieve;
the model exclusions, S₆ contradiction and A.3.18 residual alternatives
remain separate obligations.

Source: Aschbacher–Smith, combined manuscript of 1 March 2004,
proof of 13.6.6, printed pp.926–927; 1.1.3–4 and 1.2.1(3).
-/

@[expose] public section
namespace CFSG.Quasithin.Chapter13.Section06.CentralizerReduction.ComponentReduction.InitialSieve
